 problems  affecting heat water food and medicine  and as jackson proskow reports it didn't  have to be this way  after days without heat texans are  coming home to heartbreak  our whole apartment burst pipes water  still undrinkable there's no dairy no  frozen vegetables  while grocery stores run dry pretty  short on supplies especially water  right now the search for warmth has led  to tragedy  hundreds of cases of carbon monoxide  poisoning  and fires this one killed three children  and their grandmother  for this natural disaster officials are  facing blame  for failing to protect the power grid  from a situation just like this  the warnings have been there for years  and  governor abbott has done nothing before  that governor perry did nothing  this was a train wreck on the way to  happening  10 years ago in 2011 another winter  storm prompted urgent pleas from federal  regulators for texas to winterize its  vulnerable power plants  to keep them from failing it didn't  happen  in part because texas operates on its  own power grid  unconnected to the rest of the u.s and  out of reach of federal regulations  every source of power that the state of  texas has  has been compromised the decision kept  electricity prices low  but has exacted an unimaginable cost the  state's electric grid came within  minutes of total collapse  that could have cut power for months i  don't want to raise false hope  and pretend that things are gonna get  meaningfully  better very soon five days in the dark  was enough to strip away the necessities  of life  getting life back to normal will take  much longer  jackson prosko global news bailing in  the middle of a crisis is never a good  look and there are calls tonight for  texas senator ted cruz to resign  he flew to mexico while millions of  people in his home state were suffering  photos of senator cruz at houston  airport and on an airplane rapidly  spread on social media wednesday night  so did the outrage today cruz was seen  heading home  checking in for a flight at cancun  international airport in a statement he  didn't apologize he said our girls asked  to take a trip with friends wanting to  be a good dad i  flew down with them last night and i'm  flying back this afternoon  the u.s advises against all travel to  mexico because of covid 19.  what if the novel coronavirus is here to  stay still ahead an infectious diseases  physician  offers some hope  it is hard to see where the world will  be six months or a year from now  one question that is being debated is  whether we have to get used to the idea  that the coronavirus  is here to stay in january the journal  nature asks more than 100 immunologists  infectious disease researchers and  virologists whether they think the virus  can be eradicated 89  said no they think it's very likely or  likely to become  an endemic virus meaning it will  continue to circulate in pockets of the  global population for years to come  it is a big topic of tour of course and  dr suman chakrabarty is  an infectious diseases physician at  mississauga hospital he's here to help  walk us through this doctor  one epidemiologist in the survey said  eradicating this virus from the world is  a lot like trying to plan the  construction of a stepping stone pathway  to the moon  it's unrealistic what is your view  i agree with that you know a respiratory  virus job  is to go basically go from person to  person it can happen when you have very  little  symptoms it's very very contagious in  that sense so it's not something that  you can completely eliminate but that  said it's going to change the way it  actually is over the next several months  it becomes something that's more like a  circulating virus like we see with  influenza it tends to  cause problems in the wintertime but  it's something that's not going to be  the same type of bad pandemic spread  that we're seeing right now  well is that what it means when a  disease is endemic as opposed to a  pandemic and  how does that actually affect the  population yeah when you have an endemic  disease it's something that's there  at kind of a low level of transmission  and you might have sometimes where  there's our spikes in transmission but  it's not the same as it is right now  where we have these massive spikes that  can overwhelm hospital systems  it's more something that happens you  know seasonally and we deal with this  every year when we deal with influenza  so it's something that we live with  uh it's it's uh you can do it for years  and you have a vaccine that can keep  things even better under control  and i think that's what we expect to see  in the next several years yeah well i  want to ask you about what role  vaccines will play they'll reduce the  risk of infection of course but will  they be game changers that will  really allow economies to open up  absolutely obviously it's part of the  entire picture that we're having with  our fight against the virus but vaccines  are a huge part of that  it takes severe disease and turns it  into something that's mild or a  non-existent and with that  you can have a lot of pressure taken off  the hospitals you're saving lives you're  saving morbidity and all these types of  things are our ticket out of the  pandemic  i do expect that we might need boosters  in the future but to have another  pandemic with this same virus is very  unlikely so things will  largely get back to normal as 2021 rolls  on so if the virus does become  endemic and is going to be with us what  do we do what do our  lives look like from day to day well our  lives would look  to be honest the same it's just that in  the hospitals around the winter time  december january  uh this will be part of our respiratory  virus panel that we'll look for when  people come in with respiratory symptoms  we'll have to brace for it because there  are times where there are a lot of  patients with respiratory illness that  require ventilation or hospital support  so we look for that in the health care  system in the community we don't have to  really do all that much just like you  don't do right now  but again the general things washing  your hands and that type of thing  will still be very important you know  australia and new zealand  are attempting to have zero coved and  and they lock down right now when even  one case emerges  is it possible that that could become  the norm that some countries will  effectively eradicate it and  only people who can prove they aren't  infectious will be allowed to go there  you know i think that's possible but  unlikely because at some point they have  to open their borders  they have a very significant uh  advantage to us being geographically  isolated  and that's one of the reasons they were  able to uh do this in terms of the uh  covet zero but that said that at some  point in the future they're going to be  having cove it's not going to be at a  very high level  they can deal with it with vaccination  and any cases that come in the hospitals  will be able to deal with it  overall so it'll be back largely to the  same uh  style of life that was pretty pandemic  well that's a positive message dr suman  chakrabarti thank you so much for your  expertise  thanks so much for having me take care  news blackout what facebook's battle  with the australian government could  mean  for canada  when australians checked facebook for  news this morning they got nothing  the company is in an escalating dispute  with the government which has proposed a  law that would result in internet giants  including facebook and google  paying media outlets for content used on  their sites  and it's not the only country rethinking  the rules about getting  big tech to pay for news redmond shannon  explains  australians knew facebook might do this  but it was still a shock  from the early hours of this morning the  social media giant began  shutting down australian access to the  pages of major news sites  facebook has even emptied aussie news  pages for  all users worldwide people will just  revolt against it like honestly like  especially the younger generation  there would just be chaos the aussie  anger stuck by facebook also  initially removing non-news pages  including charities and essential  services  this is an assault on a sovereign nation  it is an assault  on people's freedom it's all happening  because australia wants  social media sites to share some of  their huge ad revenues  with news outlets broadcasters and  newspapers have seen  ad income fall since the start of the  internet era  facebook argues it actually helps by  driving traffic  to new sites if we had actually talked  to each other  we probably would be in a much better  situation than we are today in contrast  google has struck  multi-million dollar deals with some  australian news outlets  so will the minister insist that  facebook pay  what they owe in canada now that would  be solidarity with the australians  ottawa is considering a similar law to  australia's  just last week mr mr speaker i was in  conversation  with france germany australia finland  the consequences for how news is funded  could be huge  but it doesn't actually tackle the key  issue which is  that the model we've largely had for  journalism and largely for print  newspapers  is broken that advertisers don't go to  print newspapers nearly as much as they  used to  the federal government has already  pledged 600 million dollars to help some  struggling use companies redmond shannon  global news  and that is global national for this  thursday i'm donna friesen tonight's  your canada is saint albert botanic park  in  saint albert alberta there are beautiful  spots all over this country please email  yours to viewers at globalnational.com  thanks for watching robin gill will be  here tomorrow and i'm back on saturday  with the new reality take care of  yourselves bye-bye
 

